● Let me first tackle question 3, since it's a self-contained analysis question,
  before scoping the two implementation items.

  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)

● On question 3 (perturbation modeling suitability for plastics_test):
  Technically runnable, but the same root problem as DESeq2 shows up in a
  different form. plastics_test has 945–1985 cells per condition post-QC —
  plenty for single-cell-resolution generative models (scGen, CPA, scVI-based
  conditional models), which don't need DESeq2's per-sample replication since
  they model at the cell level, not the sample level. So it wouldn't crash or
  refuse to fit.

  The real issue: with exactly 1 sample per condition, "condition" and "sample"
  are perfectly confounded — every cell in plastic50 came from the one
  NP_50ug_12hr library. Any perturbation effect the model learns is inseparable
  from that sample's own technical/batch signature (its own capture efficiency,
  ambient RNA profile, sequencing batch, etc.), not necessarily the chemical's
  biological effect. This is the same issue you hit with DESeq2 today, just
  showing up as unfalsifiable attribution instead of unfittable dispersion — a
  model that seems to fit well or produce clean-looking predicted shifts could
  still be modeling "which library" rather than "what response," and there'd be
  no way to tell the two apart from this data alone. I'd treat any
  perturbation-modeling results from this dataset as
  exploratory/hypothesis-generating only, not as evidence of a plastics-specific
  effect, until there are ≥2 biological replicates per condition.

  Now for items 1 and 2 — these are related (Milo and scCODA both need the same
  "control condition" designation as the DESeq2 fix) and touch a lot of surface:
  new config vars, CLI flags, a real restructuring of Stage 5a/5e's
  per-cell-type contrast logic, and two new optional dependencies across Python
  and R. Let me look at a couple more things before proposing a concrete plan.

