● User approved Claude's plan
  ⎿  Plan saved to: ~/.claude/plans/peppy-spinning-octopus.md · /plan to edit
     Multi-condition DEG + scCODA/Milo differential abundance

     Context

     plastics_test surfaced a real gap: downstream.R's DESeq2 stage
     (run_deseq2())
     runs design = ~ cond_col and calls results(dds, alpha=0.05) with no
     contrast/
     name — for a >2-level condition column, DESeq2 silently returns only the
     last-vs-first-alphabetical coefficient (e.g. plastic50 vs cocktail for
     plastics_test, never touching the real Control_12hr sample). This is the
     same
     class of silent-wrong-grouping bug CLAUDE.md already documents for
     PIPELINE_COLS/scDblFinder_class — it produces a table that looks complete
     but answers the wrong scientific question. The user wants:

     1. A user-designated control condition, so multi-level conditions get
     explicit vs-control contrasts instead of an arbitrary reference.
     2. scCODA (compositional cell-type-abundance) and Milo (kNN-neighborhood
     abundance) differential-abundance analyses, which need the same control
     designation.
     3. (Answered inline, no code) — perturbation modeling (scGen/CPA-style) is
     technically fittable on plastics_test (945–1985 cells/condition, plenty for
     cell-level models) but scientifically unreliable here for the same root
     reason DESeq2 fails: 1 sample per condition means condition and sample
     identity are perfectly confounded, so no model can separate "chemical
     effect" from "which library." Not part of this implementation.

     Confirmed with user: (a) a multi-level condition column with no
     CONTROL_CONDITION set must hard-fail with a clear error, not silently fall
     back to alphabetical (breaking change vs. today, intentional); (b) GSEA
     expands
     to run per-contrast, accepting the ~(n_levels-1)x runtime cost.

     Design decisions

     - New config var CONTROL_CONDITION (config.sh/my_project.sh, default
     ""). Threaded through run_pipeline.sh as --control-condition to both
     downstream.R and the new differential_abundance.py. Empty string means
     "not set."
     - Validation rule (duplicated in both languages, like detect_condition_col
     already is conceptually meant to be): after auto-detecting the condition
     column, if it has >2 levels and CONTROL_CONDITION is empty →
     stop()/sys.exit(1) with an error listing the detected levels and telling
     the user to set CONTROL_CONDITION in my_project.sh. If it has exactly 2
     levels, CONTROL_CONDITION stays optional (if set, still relevels for
     correct fold-change sign/direction; if unset, keeps today's behavior
     unchanged — no regression for existing 2-level runs, e.g. the existing
     5k_human_PBMC sex-confounded dataset).
     - Contrast naming: <level>_vs_<control_condition>, sanitized the same way
     cell types already are (gsub("[^A-Za-z0-9_]", "_", ...)). DEG/GSEA/Milo
     tables become DEG_<celltype>__<level>_vs_<control>.tsv, etc. (double
     underscore separates cell type from contrast, consistent with the existing
     gsub sanitizer already used for cell-type names).
     - Figure-splitting precedent: this pipeline already splits a figure into
     multiple files when one static page can't legibly hold every dimension
     (umap_overview.pdf → umap_leiden.pdf + umap_cell_type.pdf, precisely to
     avoid legend/panel overflow). Apply the same rule here instead of cramming
     cell-type × contrast × collection into one page:
       - 04_deg_summary.pdf → one file per non-control level:
     04_deg_summary__<level>_vs_<control>.pdf (bar chart per cell type, as
     today).
       - 08_gsea_summary.pdf → same split:
     08_gsea_summary__<level>_vs_<control>.pdf.
       - 05_volcano_top_celltype.pdf stays a single file — global best
     (cell_type, contrast) pair by DEG count, contrast named in the title.
       - Milo beeswarm (09_milo_beeswarm.pdf) → same per-level split,
     09_milo_beeswarm__<level>_vs_<control>.pdf.
       - scCODA is the one exception: its whole point is a single joint model
     across all condition levels at once (one ~ C(condition, Treatment(ref))
     fit yields every non-control effect simultaneously) — splitting it per
     level would misrepresent the statistics, so sccoda_effects.tsv /
     10_sccoda_effects.pdf stay singular, faceted internally by level.
     - all_results restructuring in downstream.R: change from a list keyed
     by cell type alone to a list keyed by "<cell_type>||<level>_vs_<control>",
     plus a small contrasts data frame (cell_type, level, control, key) used by
     the volcano/summary/GSEA code to group and facet. run_gsea()'s loop and
     make_downstream_summary() both iterate over this same keyed structure
     instead of over cell types directly.

     File-by-file changes

     config.sh / my_project.sh — add CONTROL_CONDITION="" near
     METADATA_FILE, with a comment matching the style of existing comments
     (explain the hard-fail rule for >2 levels).

     run_pipeline.sh
     - Add CONTROL_CONDITION="${CONTROL_CONDITION:-}" to the defaults block
     (same pattern as SKIP_AMBIENT_QC).
     - run_r_downstream(): append --control-condition "$CONTROL_CONDITION" to
     the existing Rscript downstream.R call (no other changes —
     verify_r_outputs's
     find ... -name 'DEG_*.tsv' glob still matches the new
     __<level>_vs_<control>
     suffixed filenames unchanged).
     - New run_sccoda() function, modeled on run_scanpy()'s conda-run pattern,
     called right after verify_scanpy_outputs inside run_scanpy() (or as its
     own step immediately after — "Stage 4b"), before run_r_downstream(). Reads
     only obs_metadata.csv (already written by Stage 2-4), so no dependency on
     the h5ad. Failure here (missing control on >2 levels) aborts the whole
     pipeline via set -o pipefail, which is an acceptable/expected early exit
     given the user's hard-fail choice.
     - Log lines for CONTROL_CONDITION alongside the existing Input mode: /
     Matrix dir: log block in main().

     New: differential_abundance.py (Stage 4b, Python/scrna_py env) — new
     top-level script, same shape as preprocess_qc.R (a standalone stage script
     invoked directly by run_pipeline.sh, not imported by scanpy_analysis.py).
     - CLI: --scanpy-dir, --tables-dir, --viz-dir, --control-condition.
     - Reads obs_metadata.csv only (sample, cell_type, and raw metadata columns
     already merged in by scanpy_analysis.py).
     - Duplicates a small PIPELINE_COLS/detect_condition_col() (Python port of
     the R version in downstream.R) — CLAUDE.md gets a note that these two
     copies (R + Python) must be kept in sync, extending the existing
     PIPELINE_COLS warning.
     - try/except ImportError around sccoda (optional dep, degrades gracefully
     like scrublet/harmonypy/celltypist) — log and exit 0 (this stage is
     optional-module-graceful, not pipeline-fatal, except for the shared
     control-condition hard-fail rule which is intentionally fatal).
     - Fits one sccoda.util.comp_ana model on sample × cell_type counts with
     condition releveled so control_condition is the reference; scCODA
     auto-selects its reference cell type (its own established heuristic — no
     new config var needed).
     - Writes sccoda_effects.tsv (tidy: cell_type, condition_level, effect,
     credible flag) to --tables-dir and 10_sccoda_effects.pdf to --viz-dir,
     reusing PALETTE_QUALITATIVE/PAGE_W_IN/PAGE_H_IN/Liberation Sans
     constants duplicated the same way scanpy_analysis.py already does today.

     downstream.R
     - option_list: add --control-condition (character, default "").
     - After detect_condition_col(): validate the hard-fail rule described
     above.
     - Rewrite run_deseq2()'s inner run_one(ct): when >2 levels, relevel
     pb_meta[[cond_col]] with control_condition as reference and loop
     results(dds, contrast = c(cond_col, level, control_condition)) over every
     non-control level, returning one result per (cell_type, contrast) instead
     of
     one per cell_type. 2-level case collapses to today's single contrast
     (correctly oriented against control if set).
     - Update the volcano-pick, run_gsea(), and make_downstream_summary() to
     iterate the new keyed structure and split into per-contrast files as
     described above.
     - New run_milo() function (Stage 5f), same shape/placement as
     run_monocle3()/run_liana(): requireNamespace("miloR") guard; only runs
     if sce is in scope (i.e., the zellkonverter/h5ad path — the
     counts_matrix.csv
     fallback branch has no PCA coordinates, so Milo is skipped there with a log
     line, same graceful-degrade pattern already used elsewhere). Builds the kNN
     graph on reducedDim(sce, "X_pca_harmony") if present else "X_pca"
     (mirrors scanpy_analysis.py's own use_rep fallback) — both already land
     in the h5ad via adata.obsm, no new Python-side export needed. Loops
     testNhoods(..., model.contrasts = paste0(cond_col, level)) per non-control
     level after the same releveling as DESeq2, writes
     milo_DA_<level>_vs_<control>.tsv +
     09_milo_beeswarm__<level>_vs_<control>.pdf.
     - Call run_milo() from main alongside the other Stage 5 modules.

     envs/python_env.yaml — add sccoda under the pip section (verified
     available on conda-forge, but installing via the same pip block as
     scanpy/scrublet/etc. keeps one dependency-resolution path, consistent with
     the
     file's current all-pip-except-numpy/pandas/scipy/matplotlib structure).

     envs/r_env.yaml — add bioconductor-milor (verified on bioconda, r43
     build compatible with the pinned r-base=4.3) directly to the dependency
     list
     — unlike monocle3/liana/NMF, this one installs cleanly via conda, so no
     "install manually" note needed.

     CLAUDE.md / README.md
     - New "Multi-condition analysis" subsection: CONTROL_CONDITION, the
     hard-fail
     rule, contrast naming convention, and the figure-split rule.
     - Data contract table: add Stage 4b (differential_abundance.py) and Stage
     5f
     (Milo module in downstream.R).
     - config.sh reference table: add CONTROL_CONDITION row.
     - README output-structure tree: add
     sccoda_effects.tsv/10_sccoda_effects.pdf,
     milo_DA_*.tsv/09_milo_beeswarm__*.pdf, and update the DEG_*/GSEA_*/
     04_deg_summary*/08_gsea_summary* entries to show the __<level>_vs_<control>
     suffix pattern. Package rationale tables get sccoda (Python) and miloR (R)
     rows.

