❯ Are SoupX/scDblFinder removing cells at some point? Why aren't they applied   
  to separate matrix file?                                                      

  Read 1 file (ctrl+o to expand)

● Both are answerable directly from preprocess_qc.R's code — let me walk through
  each.

  Do SoupX/scDblFinder remove cells?

  No, neither does. Looking at the actual code:

  - SoupX (adjustCounts(channel, roundToInt=TRUE), line 84) only corrects
  gene×cell count values — subtracting the estimated ambient-RNA contamination —
  on the exact same set of barcodes as the input filtered matrix.
  write10xCounts() then writes that corrected matrix out with the same cell
  count. No columns (cells) are dropped.
  - scDblFinder just computes scDblFinder.score/scDblFinder.class per cell and
  writes them to doublet_calls.tsv (lines 108-117) — it flags, it doesn't
  filter. This matches CLAUDE.md's explicit design: "Doublets are flagged, not 
  removed." Actual cell filtering happens later and separately, in
  scanpy_analysis.py's MAD-based is_outlier() — and even there, nothing filters
  on the doublet columns automatically; they just ride along in .obs for
  inspection.

  One indirect wrinkle worth knowing: since SoupX changes count values (not
  which cells exist), a cell whose corrected total_counts/n_genes drops enough
  as a result could subsequently get flagged as a MAD outlier in Stage 2 — but
  that's a downstream consequence of the corrected numbers, not something
  SoupX/scDblFinder do themselves.

  Why don't they run on matrix-mode input?

  Two separate reasons, one hard and one incidental:

  1. SoupX structurally needs the raw/unfiltered matrix — SoupChannel(raw$mat, 
  filt$mat, ...) (line 75) estimates the ambient RNA profile from empty-droplet
  background in the unfiltered matrix. --raw-dir is NULL by default and SoupX is
  skipped without it (line 89-90). Matrix mode's flat layout
  (MATRIX_DIR/<sample>/{matrix.mtx.gz,features.tsv.gz,barcodes.tsv.gz}) is just
  the one pre-filtered matrix — there's no equivalent raw/background matrix
  expected or read in that mode, so there's nothing to feed SoupX regardless.
  2. scDblFinder doesn't strictly need the raw matrix — but it's bundled into
  the same preprocess_qc.R stage as SoupX, and run_pipeline.sh gates that whole
  stage on INPUT_MODE == fastq (run_preprocess_qc is never called in matrix
  mode). So scDblFinder gets skipped too, even though nothing about it
  technically requires Cell Ranger's raw output — that's an architectural
  bundling choice, not a hard technical constraint like SoupX's.

